Quadrupole motion and explicit voltage conventions (@gas-qms-motion: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @industrial-gases-and-vacuum, #source "../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M", #industrial-gases-and-vacuum "Fragmentation-patterns-as-a-response-matrix.lingua#r_4YV5WH8AA3BQAM1PKGYBW52746") { r_59T7RTEYGVJ5PQP29AZ9YNX6FS
**Read:** [Total and Partial Pressure Measurement — Karl Jousten](../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M), §4.2 and the field/stability diagrams. Derive the following ideal-field convention independently before comparing numerical coefficients in other conventions.

Let Φ(x,y,t)=[U+V cos(Ωt)](x²−y²)/r₀² and ion charge Q>0, mass m. Newton's equation gives ẍ+[2Q/(mr₀²)](U+V cos Ωt)x=0 and the opposite sign for y. With ξ=Ωt/2, write x″+[ax−2qx cos(2ξ)]x=0, where ax=8QU/(mr₀²Ω²) and qx=−4QV/(mr₀²Ω²); ay=−ax and qy=−qx. A time-origin shift can reverse the RF sign without changing stability.

The potential explicitly fixes whether U,V mean rod-to-axis amplitudes or a rod-to-rod difference. Confusing these conventions introduces factors of two; substituting frequency f for angular frequency Ω introduces factors of 2π. Both transverse motions must remain bounded long enough for ions to reach the detector.

Finite rod length, nonideal fields, entrance conditions and collisions influence transmission and resolution. A stability diagram describes ideal motion; it does not alone predict an actual instrument's peak heights.

**Work:** Derive the coefficients and units, then integrate trajectories with independently checked time steps. Compare both axes and distinguish finite observation time from true asymptotic stability. Explain why a numerical coefficient copied without its voltage and length conventions is not reproducible physics.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
